message.content:
{
    "short_answer": "Sir William Bowman",
    "long_answer": "Sir William Bowman (1816–1892) was the English surgeon-anatomist whose microscopic studies of the kidney, including the description of Bowman's capsule, furnished the pathological basis for Bright’s disease (glomerulonephritis). Bowman died on 29 March 1892—the same year in which Italian-American painter Domenico Tojetti passed away—making him the historical figure who both described Bright’s disease and shares Tojetti’s death year."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Lead poisoning is a well-established cause of peripheral motor neuropathy (classically wrist-drop or foot-drop due to axonal degeneration). However, hypopnea—abnormally shallow breathing that is usually discussed in the context of sleep-disordered breathing or central respiratory depression—is not a recognized or commonly reported manifestation of lead toxicity in either the occupational-medicine or toxicology literature. Standard toxicology references list gastrointestinal colic, anemia, nephropathy, encephalopathy, cognitive deficits, and peripheral neuropathy as major effects, but they do not cite hypopnea or respiratory-center depression as part of the clinical picture. Therefore, while lead toxicity can cause peripheral neuropathy, it is not considered a causative factor for hypopnea, so the combined statement is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Naltrexone",
    "long_answer": "Naltrexone, an opioid-receptor antagonist approved for alcohol and opioid dependence, has been reported in case series and small studies to reduce symptoms in patients with chronic spontaneous urticaria (chronic hives). Low-dose naltrexone modulates opioid receptors on mast cells and immune cells, dampening histamine release and inflammation, which can alleviate persistent hives when conventional antihistamines fail."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aotus monkey",
    "long_answer": "The Aotus, or owl monkey, can be experimentally infected with Plasmodium falciparum—the principal malaria parasite in humans—allowing researchers to study human-type malaria pathogenesis, immunity, vaccines and antimalarial drugs in a non-human primate model."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shortened diastole",
    "long_answer": "A high heart rate markedly abbreviates diastole—the phase when mesenteric vessels normally receive the bulk of their blood flow. The resulting reduction in diastolic perfusion time lowers mesenteric arterial flow and oxygen delivery, predisposing the bowel to hypoperfusion and acute intestinal ischemia, especially in patients with marginal splanchnic circulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adrenocortical carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "A malignant tumor of the adrenal cortex, such as an adrenocortical carcinoma, can infiltrate and destroy the zona glomerulosa. Loss of this aldosterone-producing tissue leads to markedly reduced aldosterone synthesis, producing primary hypoaldosteronism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant disorder",
    "long_answer": "Wells-Jankovic syndrome is a familial movement-disorder (blepharospasm with oromandibular dystonia) that shows vertical transmission through successive generations and equal sex involvement; genetic studies of affected families demonstrate single-allele (heterozygous) segregation, establishing it as an autosomal dominant condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankyrin",
    "long_answer": "Ankyrin-1, the membrane adaptor most frequently mutated in hereditary spherocytosis, interacts with the cytoplasmic loop of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase α-subunit that contains its kinase (ATP-binding/nucleotide-binding) domain. By binding at this ATP-binding region, ankyrin links the pump—and other membrane proteins such as Band 3—to the spectrin cytoskeleton; loss of functional ankyrin therefore destabilises the red-cell membrane and produces hereditary spherocytosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AIS Sports Supplement Framework",
    "long_answer": "The Australian Institute of Sport (AIS) Sports Supplement Framework lists creatine monohydrate in its Group A “performance supplements” category, describing its primary mechanism as enhancing muscle energy metabolism by increasing phosphocreatine availability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis",
    "long_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis is a systemic inflammatory joint disease that frequently produces secondary Raynaud phenomenon through small-vessel vasculitis and autonomic dysregulation. The same disease is a well-recognized predisposing factor for complex regional pain syndrome (historically called reflex sympathetic dystrophy or shoulder-hand syndrome) because persistent joint inflammation or minor trauma in RA can trigger the chronic neuropathic pain, autonomic, and trophic changes that define CRPS. Thus rheumatoid arthritis is associated with both Raynaud syndrome and complex regional pain syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MDA5",
    "long_answer": "During alphavirus replication, long double-stranded RNA intermediates accumulate in the cytoplasm. The pattern-recognition receptor MDA5 binds this viral dsRNA and signals through MAVS, activating IRF3/7 and NF-κB. This signaling cascade induces transcription and secretion of type I interferons, initiating the antiviral response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-6 (IL-6)",
    "long_answer": "Numerous clinical studies have shown that serum levels of the pro-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-6 (IL-6) are markedly higher in patients experiencing active uveitis than in healthy controls. IL-6, produced by T cells, macrophages and ocular resident cells, drives Th17 differentiation and promotes the inflammatory cascade characteristic of uveitis, making its elevation a consistent systemic biomarker of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinoblastoma",
    "long_answer": "Survivors of hereditary retinoblastoma, especially those treated with cranial radiotherapy, have a markedly increased incidence of secondary intracranial tumors, with meningiomas being among the most common. The combination of germ-line RB1 mutations and prior radiation exposure confers this elevated meningioma risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DermAtlas",
    "long_answer": "DermAtlas is an open-access dermatology image database maintained by the Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine. It hosts thousands of clinical and histopathologic photographs, and its catalog specifically contains image sets illustrating ichthyosis vulgaris and related skin disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary hypertension",
    "long_answer": "A large, long-standing atrial septal defect produces a chronic left-to-right shunt that can lead to pulmonary arterial hypertension and marked dilation of the main pulmonary artery. The enlarged pulmonary artery may compress the adjacent left main coronary artery or otherwise impair coronary perfusion, thereby substantially increasing the risk of clinically significant coronary artery disease in patients with an atrial septal defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the PAX3 gene are well-established causes of Waardenburg syndrome types 1 and 3 and related craniofacial-deafness-hand syndromes. Cohen syndrome, however, is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the VPS13B (COH1) gene; PAX3 is not implicated in Cohen syndrome in any reputable clinical or genetic studies. Likewise, ocular coloboma is most often associated with mutations in genes such as PAX2, PAX6, CHD7, RAX, SHH, and several others, but PAX3 is not recognized as a causative gene for coloboma in the medical genetics literature. Therefore, the assertion that PAX3 mutations are associated with Cohen syndrome and are a cause of coloboma is not supported by established scientific consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Langer–Giedion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Langer–Giedion syndrome (also called trichorhinophalangeal syndrome type II) is produced by a contiguous-gene deletion on chromosome 8q24 that encompasses TRPS1 and EXT1. Clinically, affected individuals show characteristic facial features, which prominently include micrognathism (micrognathia), along with sparse hair, a bulbous nasal tip, and multiple exostoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR, also called ERBB1/HER1) was first identified in the late-1970s/early-1980s through biochemical purification and radioligand-binding studies of the epidermal growth factor (EGF) receptor protein in cell lines (e.g., A431 cells). Cloning of the EGFR cDNA quickly followed. Its discovery was not the result of a chromosomal translocation study, nor was it traced to any pedigree, Italian or otherwise. Although rearrangements involving EGFR have since been described in certain tumors, the original identification of the gene and receptor had nothing to do with a familial chromosomal translocation. Therefore, the statement is not consistent with the established historical record."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratolytic therapy",
    "long_answer": "Lichen spinulosus is characterized by follicular hyper-keratosis, producing spiny plugs of excess stratum corneum. Topical or procedural keratolytic therapy (e.g., salicylic acid, urea, lactic acid, retinoids, gentle dermabrasion) breaks down keratin and exfoliates the thickened stratum corneum, thereby removing the excess skin and improving the lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncomodulin",
    "long_answer": "After an axon is injured and undergoes Wallerian degeneration, blood-derived macrophages invade the distal nerve stump. These macrophages secrete the small calcium-binding protein oncomodulin. Oncomodulin binds to receptors on injured neurons, elevates intracellular cAMP, and potently stimulates growth‐associated signaling pathways, thereby promoting the elongation and regeneration of axons. Hence, oncomodulin is the macrophage-derived protein critical for axonal regeneration following Wallerian degeneration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachydactyly",
    "long_answer": "Cooks syndrome is characterised by under-development or absence of several phalangeal bones, producing abnormally short fingers. This skeletal shortening of the digits is the classical hand manifestation known as brachydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Concern",
    "long_answer": "Donald Winnicott noted that when an infant first recognizes the caregiver as an external, separate object (transition from simple object-relating to true object-use), the characteristic affect that appears is ‘concern’: the infant now worries about the well-being of the object it has previously attacked in fantasy and begins to feel responsible and to wish to repair. This emotion marks the developmental milestone of acknowledging an outside object’s independent existence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatology",
    "long_answer": "Tietze syndrome is an inflammatory disorder of the costochondral junction—a musculoskeletal and connective-tissue problem. Diseases of this type are routinely evaluated within rheumatology, and rheumatologists are the specialists most often consulted to distinguish Tietze syndrome from other causes of chest pain and to confirm the diagnosis on the basis of clinical and imaging findings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic megaduodenum",
    "long_answer": "When the marked dilatation of the duodenum (megaduodenum) cannot be attributed to any demonstrable mechanical obstruction, neuropathy, or systemic disease, it is classified as ‘idiopathic megaduodenum.’ The term ‘idiopathic’ denotes that the underlying cause is unknown or primary, distinguishing it from secondary forms due to identifiable conditions such as congenital atresia, malrotation, Chagas disease, or scleroderma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jejunum",
    "long_answer": "Niacin (vitamin B3) and its precursor tryptophan are chiefly absorbed in the jejunum. Inflammation of the jejunum impairs this absorption, producing niacin deficiency and the clinical syndrome of pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inhibin",
    "long_answer": "Inhibin is a dimeric glycoprotein complex (α- and β-subunits) that provides negative feedback on pituitary FSH release and is therefore a key regulator of follicular development and the menstrual cycle. The genes encoding its subunits (INHA and INHBB) lie close to the centromere of chromosome 2, a region that is recurrently duplicated in small supernumerary marker chromosomes found in otherwise karyotypically normal men and women with unexplained infertility. Extra copies of these inhibin genes carried on the sSMCs are thought to disturb the normal FSH–inhibin feedback loop, making Inhibin the protein complex specifically linked to sSMC-associated infertility cases." }

{
    "short_answer": "Positron emission tomography",
    "long_answer": "Source amnesia in Alzheimer’s disease has been investigated with positron emission tomography (PET), which measures regional cerebral glucose metabolism or blood flow while patients perform source-memory tasks. PET studies have shown reduced activation in prefrontal and medial temporal regions during these tasks, linking the metabolic dysfunction detected by PET to the source-memory deficit characteristic of Alzheimer’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitral valve",
    "long_answer": "In Fabry disease, deficiency of α-galactosidase A causes globotriaosylceramide (a sphingolipid) to accumulate in cardiac tissues. The valve leaflets most often affected are those of the mitral valve—particularly the posterior leaflet—leading to leaflet thickening and mitral regurgitation, a characteristic cardiac manifestation of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leu206Arg (L206R)",
    "long_answer": "Carney complex is linked to dysregulated cyclic-AMP/PKA signalling. The best-characterised amino-acid change is a leucine-to-arginine substitution at position 206 (L206R) in the catalytic subunit α of protein kinase A (PRKACA). This gain-of-function mutation increases PKA activity, driving the multiple endocrine and cutaneous tumours that typify Carney complex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "In fibrous dysplasia the over-expressed phosphaturic hormone is fibroblast growth factor-23 (FGF23), which is secreted in excess by the pathologic bone marrow stromal/osteogenic cells and can cause hypophosphatemia. There is no evidence that fibroblast growth factor-21 (FGF21) is over-produced in fibrous dysplasia; FGF21 is primarily a metabolic regulator produced by liver and adipose tissue rather than bone stromal cells. Consequently, the statement that FGF21 is excessively produced by bone marrow stromal cells in fibrous dysplasia is not supported by established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nelson syndrome",
    "long_answer": "After bilateral adrenalectomy for Cushing’s disease, the loss of cortisol-mediated negative feedback allows the pituitary corticotroph adenoma to enlarge and secrete massive amounts of ACTH and MSH, producing hyperpigmentation, headaches, and visual field defects. This post-adrenalectomy pituitary tumor growth with very high ACTH levels is known as Nelson syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypomelanosis of Ito",
    "long_answer": "Hypomelanosis of Ito (also called incontinentia pigmenti achromians) is a neurocutaneous syndrome distinguished by streaked or whorled hypopigmentation of the skin. Ocular manifestations are common and include microphthalmia, strabismus and nystagmus, with the latter arising from the abnormal visual pathway development associated with the pigmentary defect. Thus, the syndrome that combines hypopigmentation, microphthalmia and nystagmus is Hypomelanosis of Ito."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ureteral duplication",
    "long_answer": "Michelin tire baby syndrome (multiple symmetric circumferential skin creases) is sometimes accompanied by genitourinary malformations, most notably duplication of the ureter (a double or bifid ureter). This congenital anomaly of the ureter has been described in several reported cases of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-6",
    "long_answer": "Cardiac myxomas and other primary cardiac tumors often secrete the pro-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-6 (IL-6). Tumor-derived IL-6 enters the circulation, driving an acute-phase response that produces fever, weight loss, elevated ESR/CRP and autoantibody formation, thereby explaining the systemic inflammatory and autoimmune-like manifestations seen in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pica",
    "long_answer": "Children with Sanfilippo syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type III) frequently develop pica—compulsive ingestion of non-nutritive substances such as paper, dirt or cloth. This behavior is well documented in clinical series of Sanfilippo patients and is attributed to the neurodegenerative and behavioral manifestations of the disorder, making pica the eating disorder most commonly reported in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chief complaint",
    "long_answer": "In clinical practice, the first symptom or concern that prompts a patient to seek medical attention—such as abdominal bloating or pelvic pain in ovarian cancer—is recorded as the chief complaint. It represents the patient’s primary reason for the medical visit and guides the initial diagnostic work-up."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Generally good",
    "long_answer": "Onychomycosis is rarely life-threatening and, with appropriate systemic or topical antifungal therapy, most patients can achieve clinical and mycological cure. However, treatment often requires months, the cure rate is only 60–80 %, and relapse or reinfection is common (15–30 % within a few years). Thus the overall prognosis is favorable for health and nail function, but prolonged therapy and vigilance for recurrence are necessary."
}

{
    "short_answer": "René Laënnec",
    "long_answer": "René Théophile Hyacinthe Laënnec, the French physician who later invented the stethoscope (1816), first gave a detailed clinical description of spontaneous pneumothorax in 1803 while still a young physician. His subsequent work on auscultation and lung disease built on this early observation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stimulus-sensitive spasms",
    "long_answer": "In stiff-person syndrome the core or primary feature is continuous muscle rigidity produced by involuntary co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles. Superimposed on this, patients experience intermittent, painful muscle contractions that are precipitated by sudden noise, touch, or emotional stress. These episodes are termed stimulus-sensitive (episodic) muscle spasms and are regarded as the secondary phenomenon accompanying the primary stiffness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachydactyly index",
    "long_answer": "Clinicians diagnose brachydactyly by calculating the Brachydactyly Index (BI). The BI compares the combined length of selected fingers—classically the 2nd, 3rd and 4th—to overall hand length (measured on a hand radiograph or by direct anthropometry). When this ratio falls below established normative thresholds, it indicates that the fingers are disproportionately short relative to the hand, confirming brachydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes insipidus nephrogenicus",
    "long_answer": "In medical Latin, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is termed “Diabetes insipidus nephrogenicus.” The phrase keeps the standard core name “diabetes insipidus” (a water-diuresis disorder) and adds the adjective “nephrogenicus,” indicating that the defect originates in the kidney (nephros), where the renal tubules are unresponsive to antidiuretic hormone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematoxylin–eosin stain",
    "long_answer": "Definitive diagnosis of primary cardiac tumors relies on histopathologic examination of excised or biopsied tissue. The routine hematoxylin–eosin (H&E) stain is the standard method used, because it clearly delineates cellular and stromal architecture, allowing pathologists to identify tumor types such as myxoma, fibroma, or rhabdomyoma. Thus, H&E staining is the commonly applied technique for diagnosing primary cardiac tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Graham-Boyle-Troxell syndrome is reported to frequently present with systemic hypertension; persistently elevated arterial blood pressure is also one of the most powerful, widely recognized modifiable risk factors for both ischaemic and haemorrhagic stroke."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Patrick Couvreur",
    "long_answer": "Patrick Couvreur is a French pharmacologist who pioneered drug vectorization through polymeric and squalene-based nanoparticles. His doxorubicin-loaded poly(alkyl cyanoacrylate) nanoparticles were the first system shown to cross the blood–brain barrier and eradicate experimental gliomas, including brainstem models, thereby opening a novel therapeutic avenue for treating otherwise inoperable brainstem gliomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acidic soils",
    "long_answer": "Magnesium ions are easily displaced and leached from soils with a low pH; thus, on acidic (often sandy, highly leached) soils the available magnesium pool is depleted, making plants prone to magnesium deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CNTNAP2",
    "long_answer": "The autism-risk gene CNTNAP2 (Contactin-associated protein-like 2) has been examined with functional MRI in humans; carriers of risk variants show altered functional connectivity in frontal, language and default-mode networks, demonstrating how this gene influences brain activity patterns relevant to autism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "F8 (Factor VIII)",
    "long_answer": "The F8 gene encodes coagulation factor VIII, a key protein in the intrinsic clotting pathway. Alternative-splicing defects in F8 lower functional factor VIII levels, which prolongs the activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT). Therefore, the partial thromboplastin time test is used clinically to evaluate the functional consequences of alternative splicing in the F8 gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hantavirus infection",
    "long_answer": "Rodent-borne hantaviruses that cause hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome are zoonotic; within the first few days of illness patients often develop thrombocytopenia that produces hemorrhagic signs such as petechiae and epistaxis. Nosebleeds are therefore a characteristic early manifestation of human hantavirus infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dyslexie font",
    "long_answer": "Controlled studies have found that the specialized ‘Dyslexie’ typeface, designed to reduce letter confusion in dyslexic readers, does not improve reading speed, accuracy, or comprehension compared with standard fonts such as Arial or Times New Roman. Consequently, scientific consensus holds that the Dyslexie font is not an effective treatment for dyslexia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin D supplementation",
    "long_answer": "The hypocalcaemia caused by hypoparathyroidism in Dahlberg-Borer-Newcomer syndrome is routinely managed with oral vitamin D (often the active form, calcitriol) to enhance intestinal calcium absorption and normalize serum calcium levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Geographic retinal dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Retinal dysplasia is classically divided into focal/multifocal, geographic, and complete (generalised) forms. The geographic form is characterised by large, plaque-like areas of retinal disorganisation that compromise the local blood supply. If these lesions are not treated or monitored, the ischaemic retinal tissue can undergo degeneration and frank necrosis. Hence, necrosis is a recognised potential outcome specifically of the geographic type of retinal dysplasia when left untreated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric nephrology",
    "long_answer": "In children, fibromuscular dysplasia most commonly involves the renal arteries and presents as renovascular hypertension. Evaluation and management of renal-artery disease and childhood hypertension are typically handled by pediatric nephrologists, so this specialty is the one most often responsible for treating pediatric patients with fibromuscular dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT7A",
    "long_answer": "Tracy Barrell’s disability is classified as phocomelia, a severe limb-reduction malformation. The best-established genetic cause of human phocomelia is loss-of-function mutation of the WNT7A gene; biallelic WNT7A variants underlie Al-Awadi/Raas-Rothschild/Schinzel phocomelia and related syndromes by disrupting Wnt signalling essential for limb-bud patterning. Thus the congenital malformation is most directly associated with the WNT7A gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC8 (CSA)",
    "long_answer": "The more moderate, classic form of Cockayne syndrome (often termed Type I) is most frequently associated with mutations in the ERCC8 gene, which encodes the CSA protein involved in transcription-coupled nucleotide-excision repair. Individuals harboring ERCC8/CSA mutations typically show a milder clinical course and longer survival compared with those carrying ERCC6/CSB mutations, making ERCC8 the gene linked to the more moderate phenotype of Cockayne syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fludrocortisone",
    "long_answer": "Fludrocortisone acetate is prescribed for the hypotension and autonomic failure seen in dopamine β-hydroxylase deficiency because its mineralocorticoid action expands plasma volume and raises blood pressure. As a corticosteroid, it also has intrinsic glucocorticoid activity, so it is used clinically for its anti-inflammatory effects in a variety of inflammatory disorders. Thus the same drug can be employed in both dopamine β-hydroxylase deficiency and in the treatment of inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Specific phobia",
    "long_answer": "Genuphobia—the irrational, persistent fear of knees or kneeling—is categorized in psychiatry as a specific phobia. Specific phobias are a subgroup of anxiety disorders characterized by marked fear or anxiety triggered by a particular object or situation, leading to avoidance behavior and functional impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Five-flap Z-plasty",
    "long_answer": "To separate the fused thumb and index finger and recreate a wide, deep first web space in ectrodactyly, surgeons commonly perform a five-flap Z-plasty. This local-flap technique provides adequate skin lengthening and web-space deepening in one stage, making it the procedure of choice for correcting thumb–index syndactyly in split-hand (ectrodactyly) patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone morphogenetic protein 4",
    "long_answer": "In the developing limb bud, Bone morphogenetic protein 4 (BMP-4) is highly expressed in the mesenchyme between the nascent digits. BMP-4 activates the intrinsic apoptotic pathway in these interdigital cells, leading to their programmed cell death. This BMP-4-driven apoptosis removes the intervening tissue, allowing the fingers and toes to separate during early human fetal development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adenosine deaminase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Nezelof syndrome is a form of severe combined immunodeficiency in which the underlying molecular defect is often complete loss of the purine-catabolizing enzyme adenosine deaminase (ADA). Absence of ADA leads to accumulation of deoxy-adenosine and dATP, metabolites that are toxic to rapidly dividing lymphoid cells, producing the profound T-cell immunodeficiency characteristic of Nezelof syndrome. The same genetic ADA defect can injure alveolar septal cells and tip the protease–antiprotease balance in the lung, resulting in widespread destruction of acini and the panacinar pattern of emphysema described in ADA-deficient patients. Thus, ADA deficiency is the well-established genetic lesion common to both Nezelof syndrome and an emphysematous lung disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melphalan",
    "long_answer": "The alkylating chemotherapeutic agent melphalan has been documented to induce complete or near-complete clearing of cutaneous lesions in several reported cases of papular mucinosis (lichen myxedematosus/scleromyxedema). Although effective, its use is limited by the risk of severe bone-marrow toxicity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Europe",
    "long_answer": "Although Europe is not an endemic region for yaws, several European countries (e.g., the United Kingdom, France, the Netherlands, Germany) have documented imported or sporadic yaws cases, demonstrating that the continent has reported yaws in otherwise non-endemic settings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kleefstra syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kleefstra syndrome is caused by haplo-insufficiency of the EHMT1 gene located on chromosome 9q34.3. Patients with this 9q34 microdeletion/point-mutation syndrome present with developmental delay, characteristic facial features and multiple systemic findings; endocrine abnormalities, particularly congenital or acquired hypothyroidism, are well-described components of the phenotype. Hence the genetic disorder on 9q34 that is associated with hypothyroidism is Kleefstra syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mayo Clinic",
    "long_answer": "The first substantial clinical and electrophysiological description of Lambert–Eaton myasthenic syndrome was published in 1956 by neurologists Edward H. Lambert, Lee M. Eaton and colleagues working at the Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota. Their Mayo Clinic case series detailed the characteristic muscle weakness that improves after exercise and the impaired presynaptic acetylcholine release, establishing the disorder that now bears their names."
}

{
    "short_answer": "tyrosinase gene",
    "long_answer": "The albino research strain of the snail Biomphalaria glabrata carries a loss-of-function mutation in its tyrosinase gene (Tyr). Tyrosinase is the key rate-limiting enzyme in the melanin biosynthetic pathway; when the gene is mutated, melanin cannot be produced, resulting in the transparent, albino phenotype that facilitates laboratory studies of the parasite–snail interaction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distal phalanges",
    "long_answer": "Nicolaides–Baraitser syndrome often involves brachydactyly characterized by blunted or shortened distal (terminal) phalanges of the fingers and toes, which can be seen radiographically and contribute to the broad, stubby appearance of the digit tips."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ulrich Sigwart",
    "long_answer": "In 1994, Swiss interventional cardiologist Ulrich Sigwart first described percutaneous alcohol septal ablation, a catheter-based, non-surgical technique that reduces left-ventricular outflow obstruction in hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy. His landmark publication established alcohol septal ablation as an alternative to surgical septal myectomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sjögren’s syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Primary Sjögren’s syndrome is an autoimmune exocrinopathy that frequently produces immune-complex (mixed) cryoglobulinemia and can present with systemic vasculitis. Among its vasculitic manifestations, patients are at increased risk for large-vessel involvement, including giant cell (temporal) arteritis. Therefore, Sjögren’s syndrome is the autoimmune disease linked to both cryoglobulinemia and giant cell arteritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-6 (IL-6)",
    "long_answer": "Primary cardiac tumors—especially atrial myxomas—often secrete the pro-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-6 (IL-6). Circulating IL-6 stimulates hepatic acute-phase protein production and activates systemic immune responses, producing constitutional symptoms such as fever, malaise, weight loss, anemia, and an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate. Thus, excess IL-6 released by the tumor is responsible for the characteristic systemic inflammatory manifestations seen in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Latent HIV reservoirs",
    "long_answer": "The major obstacle to curing HIV/AIDS is the virus’s ability to establish lifelong latent reservoirs—chiefly integrated, transcriptionally silent proviral DNA in long-lived resting memory CD4+ T cells (and some other cells). Because latent HIV is invisible to both antiretroviral drugs (which target active replication) and the immune system, it persists for decades and can rekindle infection if therapy is stopped, making eradication extremely difficult."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Diabetes mellitus is by far the leading metabolic cause of peripheral neuropathy.  Although several studies have shown that some patients with impaired glucose tolerance (\"prediabetes\") can develop mild small-fiber neuropathy, major reviews and clinical guidelines (e.g., from the American Academy of Neurology, the American Diabetes Association, and UpToDate) do NOT list prediabetes among the principal or common causes of peripheral neuropathy in the general population.  In most epidemiologic series, neuropathy attributable to prediabetes accounts for only a small minority of cases, whereas longstanding diabetes, alcohol misuse, hereditary disorders, autoimmune disease, infections, and drug/toxin exposure constitute the predominant etiologies.  Therefore, current scientific consensus considers prediabetes an infrequent, not a common, cause of peripheral neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tumor-suppressive mechanism",
    "long_answer": "Because long-living multicellular animals consist of many cells that must divide for decades, they face a high lifetime risk of oncogenic mutation. Cellular senescence evolved as a tumor-suppressive mechanism in this context: when a cell senses DNA damage, telomere attrition, oncogene activation or other stresses, it irreversibly exits the cell cycle, preventing clonal expansion and cancer. Thus senescence is fundamentally tied to the requirements of multicellularity; it protects the organism early in life but, as senescent cells accumulate, their pro-inflammatory secretions promote tissue dysfunction and contribute to ageing."}

{
    "short_answer": "TOXBASE",
    "long_answer": "TOXBASE is the National Poisons Information Service’s online toxicology database that provides detailed information on the clinical features, toxicity, and management of overdoses with medicines such as the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug indometacin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Short-rib polydactyly syndrome is an autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia. Because its diagnosis relies on recognizing a congenital pattern of malformations and confirming pathogenic variants through genetic testing, and because affected families require genetic counseling and recurrence-risk assessment, the disorder is primarily evaluated and managed within the medical genetics specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PSEN1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the presenilin-1 (PSEN1) gene, a major cause of autosomal-dominant early-onset Alzheimer’s disease, have been repeatedly documented to present with extrapyramidal features resembling Parkinson’s disease (akinesia, rigidity, tremor). Certain PSEN1 variants (e.g., A431E, L235V, Y115C) produce widespread amyloid-β deposition together with Lewy body/α-synuclein pathology, clinically yielding a parkinsonism-like illness in addition to dementia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TPM2 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TPM2 (β-tropomyosin 2) gene are a well-documented cause of distal arthrogryposis, producing congenital joint contractures and muscle weakness of the limbs. These fixed contractures represent a permanent musculoskeletal impairment that is specifically listed in the Paralympic classification code, so an athlete who carries a deleterious TPM2 mutation and exhibits the associated functional limitations could be eligible to compete in the Paralympic Games."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eosinophils",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophils are the granulocytic leukocytes that mediate antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity against helminths. In onchocerciasis, IgE and other antibodies bind the surface of Onchocerca volvulus larvae (microfilariae), and eosinophils attach via Fc receptors, then degranulate, releasing major basic protein and other toxic mediators that kill the larvae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoscopic biliary stenting",
    "long_answer": "Minor iatrogenic bile duct injuries, such as small leaks after cholecystectomy, are usually managed non-operatively. The standard approach is ERCP with placement of a temporary biliary stent (often combined with sphincterotomy). The stent lowers intraductal pressure and diverts bile flow, allowing the leak to seal and the duct to heal without further surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orbicularis oris",
    "long_answer": "In Van der Woude syndrome the paramedian lower-lip pits are epithelial invaginations that penetrate through the orbicularis oris muscle; abnormal development and persistence of these invaginations in the orbicularis oris during lip fusion leads to the characteristic pits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Conductive hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "In marfanoid connective-tissue disorders the abnormal collagen weakens the tympanic membrane; when the membrane is damaged or perforated, sound transmission through the middle ear is impaired, leading to conductive hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5-alpha-reductase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Callon was reportedly raised as female but developed male-typical genitalia at puberty and then underwent surgical creation of a scrotal outlet—features classically seen in individuals with 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency. In this autosomal-recessive disorder, inadequate conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone in utero causes undervirilized external genitalia at birth, while the testosterone surge at puberty produces marked masculinization. This pattern fits the historical description of Callon’s intersex presentation and explains why early physicians attempted what is considered the first documented gender-affirming surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Copious purulent sputum",
    "long_answer": "Tracheobronchomegaly (Mounier-Kuhn syndrome) is characterized by marked dilatation of the trachea and main bronchi with loss of elastic and muscular tissue. This leads to impaired mucociliary clearance and recurrent infections, so patients typically present with a chronic productive cough that yields large volumes of purulent sputum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiocracy",
    "long_answer": "The 2006 film is Mike Judge’s “Idiocracy,” which portrays a dystopian society whose average intelligence has dramatically declined. According to the opening description of the concept of stupidity, a deficit of intelligence serves as a fundamental comedic device—stupidity is cited as a major element in humor, underpinning slapstick, satire, and other comic forms by providing situations and behaviors that audiences find funny."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VWF c.2435delC",
    "long_answer": "The single-nucleotide deletion c.2435delC in the von Willebrand factor (VWF) gene is a founder mutation among the Negev Bedouin. It causes type III von Willebrand disease and has an exceptionally high carrier rate in this population, making it the most common known genetic mutation in the Negev Bedouin community."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Todd’s paralysis",
    "long_answer": "After the focal motor seizure activity of Epilepsia partialis continua abates, patients often develop a transient, focal post-ictal weakness of the involved limb or body region. This temporary neurologic deficit is known as Todd’s paralysis and can last from minutes to several hours, reflecting brief exhaustion or inhibition of the cortical area that generated the seizure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemadsorption inhibition test",
    "long_answer": "Human parainfluenza-virus-infected cells adsorb red blood cells to their surface (hemadsorption). When type-specific antisera are added, they block this interaction; the pattern of inhibition allows clear discrimination among the different serotypes (HPIV-1, ‑2, ‑3, ‑4). Therefore, the hemadsorption inhibition test is the standard laboratory technique used to distinguish the serotypes of human parainfluenza viruses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutrophils",
    "long_answer": "Platelets form complexes with neutrophils; this interaction activates neutrophils to release neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) and enhances phagocytic killing, enabling the combined platelet-neutrophil response to efficiently destroy bacteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DrugBank",
    "long_answer": "DrugBank is a comprehensive, curated database that catalogs FDA-approved drugs and their biological targets; it lists acitretin (DrugBank ID: DB00460) together with the nuclear receptors (RARs and RXRs) it binds, providing detailed target, mechanism-of-action and pharmacological data."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AZFa region",
    "long_answer": "Microdeletions of the AZFa region on the long arm of the Y chromosome (Yq11) consistently produce a Sertoli cell-only histology and are accompanied by small, atrophic testes; thus this AZFa locus is genetically linked to both Sertoli cell-only syndrome and testicular atrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epirubicin",
    "long_answer": "Epirubicin, an anthracycline chemotherapeutic agent commonly included in regimens for gastric (stomach) cancer such as ECF (Epirubicin-Cisplatin-5-FU), is well-recognized for dose-dependent, cumulative cardiotoxicity that can lead to dilated cardiomyopathy and congestive heart failure. The drug generates free radicals and causes myocardial cell damage, explaining this characteristic adverse effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rare disease",
    "long_answer": "In the United States, Lyngstadaas syndrome is listed by the NIH Genetic and Rare Diseases Information Center (GARD) as a condition that affects a very small number of people. Under the U.S. Orphan Drug Act, disorders that affect fewer than 200,000 individuals nationwide are classified as rare diseases. Lyngstadaas syndrome meets this criterion, so it is officially considered a rare disease in the United States."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution renal ultrasonography is the standard imaging study for renal hypoplasia. It non-invasively measures kidney length, cortical thickness and overall parenchymal volume; a hypoplastic kidney appears uniformly small but maintains normal internal architecture, allowing confident diagnosis without exposing the patient to ionising radiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urinary (renal) system",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary mucoepithelial dysplasia involves abnormal mucosal epithelium in several body tracts, including the genitourinary tract. The urinary (renal) system—comprising the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra—is the body system responsible for filtering blood, eliminating metabolic waste in urine, and regulating blood volume and composition. Because HMD affects the mucosal lining of the genitourinary tract, it directly involves this urinary system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distal phalanges",
    "long_answer": "Paronychia is an infection or inflammation of the peri-ungual soft tissues around a fingernail or toenail. The bone immediately underlying the nail plate is the distal phalanx; if the infection spreads beyond the soft tissue, it can involve this bone, occasionally leading to osteomyelitis of the distal phalanx. No other bones are typically implicated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Snow–Littler procedure",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly (cleft hand) is commonly reconstructed with the Snow–Littler procedure, in which a central digit (usually the index) is transposed and the cleft closed to create a functional three-fingered hand, restore the transverse metacarpal arch, and improve pinch and grasp."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Minimizing false-negatives",
    "long_answer": "High analytical sensitivity in Fragile X genetic testing is crucial because it ensures virtually all CGG-repeat expansions and FMR1 methylation abnormalities are detected, thereby preventing false-negative results that would miss affected individuals or carriers and lead to inadequate counseling and management."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sitagliptin",
    "long_answer": "Sitagliptin, a dipeptidyl-peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitor prescribed for type 2 diabetes, has been repeatedly reported to trigger new-onset or aggravate existing psoriasis. DPP-4 is identical to the T-cell surface protein CD26; its inhibition can alter T-cell activation and cytokine release that drive psoriatic inflammation. Case reports and pharmacovigilance data show psoriasis flares appearing soon after sitagliptin initiation and resolving when the drug is discontinued, supporting its capacity to worsen the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prominent forehead",
    "long_answer": "Kowarski syndrome is a form of growth-hormone–related short stature with very low circulating IGF-1. A typical phenotypic clue is a characteristically large, protruding (prominent) forehead that stands out relative to the small facial bones and overall short stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seizures",
    "long_answer": "In anorexia nervosa, severe malnutrition and electrolyte disturbances can trigger seizures. These are sudden bursts of abnormal electrical activity in the brain that lead to involuntary muscle movements, making seizures a well-documented neurological complication of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-10 (IL-10)",
    "long_answer": "In experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis (EAE), the principal animal model of multiple sclerosis, mice lacking the anti-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-10 develop earlier onset and markedly more severe neurologic disease. IL-10 normally limits pathogenic Th1/Th17 responses and restrains myeloid-cell–mediated inflammation in the central nervous system; its genetic deficiency removes this brake, thereby exacerbating EAE pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Childhood",
    "long_answer": "MERRF (Myoclonic Epilepsy with Ragged-Red Fibers) is a mitochondrial disorder whose hallmark manifestations—myoclonus, generalized epilepsy, ataxia, and progressive muscle weakness—most often present in affected individuals during childhood (frequently in late childhood to early adolescence), before progressing over time."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tyrosinase (TYR)",
    "long_answer": "In the smooth newt, as in many other vertebrates, albinism results from a loss-of-function mutation in the tyrosinase (TYR) gene. Tyrosinase catalyzes the rate-limiting steps of melanin synthesis; when TYR is mutated, melanin cannot be produced, leading to the characteristic lack of pigmentation seen in albino individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chimpanzees",
    "long_answer": "Captive chimpanzees (Pan troglodytes) are well documented to regurgitate recently ingested food and then rechew, reswallow, or spit it out—a stereotyped ‘regurgitation-and-reingestion’ behavior that closely parallels human rumination syndrome in its timing (within minutes of eating), lack of nausea, and effortless regurgitation. Multiple behavioral studies (e.g., Hook et al., 2002, Zoo Biology) confirm this phenomenon in chimpanzees, making them the non-human primate most clearly exhibiting a rumination-like behavior."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHYH",
    "long_answer": "Most cases historically reported as Rud syndrome have been reclassified as classical Refsum disease. Classical Refsum disease is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the PHYH gene, which encodes the peroxisomal enzyme phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase. Deficiency of this enzyme blocks α-oxidation of phytanic acid, leading to the ichthyosis, neurological impairment, short stature and other manifestations that were once grouped under the name Rud syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skeletal radiographs",
    "long_answer": "A complete plain-film skeletal survey is critical because the characteristic epiphyseal-metaphyseal changes of pseudoachondroplasia and the distinctive spine-pelvis findings of achondroplasia are readily distinguished only on radiographic images, whereas clinical appearance alone can be similar."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kwashiorkor",
    "long_answer": "Kwashiorkor is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition. When dietary protein and overall caloric intake are inadequate, the body cannot maintain plasma oncotic pressure, leading to edema; it also disrupts hepatic lipid metabolism, causing a fatty liver, stunted growth, dermatoses and immunodeficiency. Thus, poor nutrition can result in kwashiorkor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intracerebral hemorrhage",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in COL4A1 that cause autosomal dominant porencephaly type I make cerebral blood-vessel walls structurally weak. Even minor head trauma can rupture these vessels, leading to intracerebral hemorrhage that enlarges porencephalic cysts and produces sudden, severe neurological damage. Therefore, avoiding head injuries is crucial to prevent potentially life-threatening brain bleeding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacillus Calmette-Guérin",
    "long_answer": "Intravesical instillation of the Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG) vaccine, best known for tuberculosis prevention and bladder cancer immunotherapy, has been studied and occasionally employed in refractory interstitial cystitis. The live attenuated mycobacteria stimulate a local immune response within the bladder wall, reducing inflammatory symptoms and pain in some patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sardinia",
    "long_answer": "Beta thalassemia is particularly common in Mediterranean populations, and epidemiological studies show that the Italian island of Sardinia has one of the highest carrier frequencies—around 10–12 % of the population—making it the Italian region with a notably high prevalence of beta thalassemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome is a congenital disorder caused by a 4p chromosomal micro-deletion. The evaluation, genetic testing, counselling of families, and coordination of multidisciplinary care for such chromosomal syndromes are core responsibilities of the medical (clinical) genetics specialty, making it the primary field involved in treating this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Jones syndrome—characterised by hereditary gingival fibromatosis accompanied by progressive sensorineural hearing loss—has been reported in multiple families with vertical transmission through successive generations, affecting males and females equally and with parent-to-child transmission. This pattern, without generation skipping or sex linkage, establishes autosomal dominant inheritance as the accepted mode of transmission for Jones syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tofacitinib citrate",
    "long_answer": "Case reports and small open-label studies have shown that oral tofacitinib citrate, a Janus kinase (JAK) 1/3 inhibitor, can reverse the autoimmune attack on hair follicles in alopecia universalis. Patients treated for several months have demonstrated not only scalp and eyebrow regrowth but also complete return of eyelashes, confirming its ability to restore eyelash growth in this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "During the first year of life",
    "long_answer": "In holoprosencephaly, most seizures first appear in infancy; cohort studies show the peak incidence occurs within the first 12 months (median onset ≈9 months), making the first year of life the period of greatest seizure risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Prenatally detected choroid plexus cysts can be a soft marker for chromosomal aneuploidy (most notably trisomy 18). Counseling about the associated genetic risks and available diagnostic options (e.g., amniocentesis, karyotyping) is typically handled by a medical genetics specialist, who is trained to provide comprehensive genetic counseling to expectant parents."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PEX1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the PEX1 gene, which encodes peroxisomal biogenesis factor-1, cause Zellweger-spectrum peroxisomal biogenesis disorders. These disorders characteristically include congenital microcephaly together with the biochemical and clinical hallmarks of peroxisomal dysfunction, linking PEX1 mutations to both microcephaly and peroxisomal disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Modified Ziehl–Neelsen stain",
    "long_answer": "Nocardia species, the causative agents of nocardiosis, are weakly acid-fast, branching, filamentous bacteria. They retain carbol-fuchsin when decolorized with a mild (≈1 % H₂SO₄) acid in the modified Ziehl–Neelsen (Kinyoun/Fite) acid-fast stain, enabling their clear visualization in clinical specimens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCA1 (ATP-binding cassette transporter A1)",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypoalphalipoproteinemia, typified by Tangier disease, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ABCA1 gene. ABCA1 acts as the key cell-surface receptor/transporter that transfers cholesterol and phospholipids to apoA-I, initiating nascent HDL formation. When ABCA1 is defective, this cholesterol efflux step fails, HDL particles are not produced, and plasma HDL-cholesterol levels fall to extremely low values—hence the association of the ABCA1 receptor with hypoalphalipoproteinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anophthalmia",
    "long_answer": "Anophthalmia is a congenital, usually genetic, malformation in which one or both eyes fail to develop. Mutations in eye-development genes such as SOX2, OTX2, PAX6 and others disrupt normal ocular formation, leaving the fellow eye frequently small and structurally abnormal (microphthalmic, with coloboma or retinal dysplasia). These defects weaken retinal integrity and significantly increase the lifetime risk of rhegmatogenous or tractional retinal detachment in the remaining eye."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD4+ T helper cells",
    "long_answer": "In systemic scleroderma the skin and other tissues are infiltrated by CD4-positive T helper lymphocytes. These cells secrete profibrotic cytokines such as TGF-β, IL-4 and IL-13, which stimulate resident fibroblasts to activate and synthesize excessive type I and III collagen, leading to the characteristic widespread fibrosis of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Single umbilical artery",
    "long_answer": "Both sirenomelia (mermaid syndrome) and omphalocele are characteristically accompanied by a single umbilical artery instead of the normal paired arteries. In sirenomelia, the persisting vitelline artery ‘steals’ blood from the caudal embryo, producing the limb fusion and renal-genitourinary defects and resulting in a single umbilical artery. Omphaloceles also frequently present with a single umbilical artery, reflecting the high incidence of associated vascular and chromosomal anomalies. Thus, the congenital vascular anomaly common to both conditions is the presence of a single umbilical artery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Symbrachydactyly",
    "long_answer": "Symbrachydactyly is a congenital limb-development disorder in which one or more digits are shortened because some or all of their phalangeal bones fail to form. The condition is classically defined by partial or complete absence of phalanges, often resulting in nubbins or missing fingers, while the metacarpals and more proximal structures are relatively preserved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IDUA",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the IDUA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme α-L-iduronidase, are the classic cause of mucopolysaccharidosis type I (Hurler syndrome). Independent pedigrees have demonstrated that IDUA loss-of-function mutations can also present primarily with progressive sensorineural deafness and retinitis pigmentosa, the defining clinical picture of Usher syndrome type III. Thus, mutation of the single gene IDUA can underlie both Hurler syndrome and Usher syndrome type III."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inferior retina",
    "long_answer": "When a coloboma involves the inferior (inferonasal) retina, the missing retinal tissue corresponds topographically to the opposite, or superior, portion of the visual field. Consequently, patients with an inferior retinal coloboma characteristically exhibit a deficit in the superior visual field."
}

{
    "short_answer": "European Neuroendocrine Tumor Society",
    "long_answer": "The European Neuroendocrine Tumor Society (ENETS) is the internationally recognized professional body that publishes evidence-based consensus guidelines on the diagnosis, staging, and multidisciplinary management of neuroendocrine tumors. Its guidelines are widely used by clinicians to standardize care across surgery, medical therapy, and follow-up for NET patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinitis pigmentosa",
    "long_answer": "Rud syndrome – a rare constellation of ichthyosis, hypogonadism, short stature and intellectual disability – has occasionally been described with degenerative changes of the retina typical of retinitis pigmentosa (night-blindness, peripheral field constriction and bone-spicule pigmentation). However, many documented patients show no retinal degeneration at all, so retinitis pigmentosa is regarded as an inconsistent, variably present ocular feature of Rud syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Misoprostol",
    "long_answer": "Misoprostol is a synthetic analogue of prostaglandin E1 (PGE1). Clinically it is administered vaginally or orally to ripen the cervix and stimulate uterine contractions, making it a standard agent for the induction of labor. Experimental and early clinical studies have also shown that misoprostol can lessen inflammatory prostaglandin signalling implicated in mood disorders; on this basis it has been investigated as an adjunctive therapy for major depressive disorder, where small trials have reported antidepressant effects. Hence the same prostaglandin analogue is utilised in both the management of major depressive disorder and the induction of labor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crouzon syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome (craniofacial dysostosis) is a genetic craniosynostosis disorder in which premature fusion of cranial sutures (usually coronal and sagittal) distorts skull and mid-facial growth. The resulting facial deformities include shallow orbits with proptosis, mid-face hypoplasia, a beaked nose, and marked hypertelorism (abnormally wide separation of the orbits). Hypertelorism is therefore a characteristic clinical sign seen in patients with Crouzon syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Cerebral creatine deficiency syndrome-1 (also called creatine transporter deficiency; OMIM #300352) is an X-linked disorder that results from pathogenic variants in the SLC6A8 gene, which encodes the creatine transporter located at Xq28. Loss-of-function mutations in SLC6A8 impair cellular uptake of creatine and lead to intellectual disability, developmental delay, seizures, and other neurologic problems.\n\nSLC6A10P, in contrast, is a pseudogene (solute carrier family 6 member 10, pseudogene). It does not encode a functional creatine transporter, is not located on the X chromosome, and there is no evidence or consensus linking variants in SLC6A10P to cerebral creatine deficiency syndrome or X-linked intellectual disability. Therefore, the claim that SLC6A10P leads to cerebral creatine deficiency syndrome-1 is not supported by established biomedical knowledge."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "In coeliac disease the immune-mediated damage to small-intestinal villi strips away brush-border enzymes, including lactase, leading to a secondary form of lactase deficiency. The same enzyme defect exists as a congenital condition (congenital alactasia); thus coeliac disease can produce an acquired mimic of this congenital small-intestinal disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Potassium hydroxide (KOH) mount",
    "long_answer": "A 10% potassium hydroxide (KOH) wet-mount of the grains or exudate dissolves host tissue and debris, allowing direct microscopic visualization of septate fungal hyphae. Demonstration of these hyphae confirms that the mycetoma is caused by true fungi (eumycetoma) rather than by actinomycetes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magpie",
    "long_answer": "The word “piebald” (and hence the medical term piebaldism) was coined from “pie,” an old English name for the magpie, whose black-and-white plumage resembles the sharply demarcated white patches of depigmented skin seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperuricemia",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen storage disease type VI, limited hepatic glycogen breakdown during fasting forces increased nucleotide turnover and ketogenesis, which reduce renal uric acid clearance. This can raise serum uric acid concentrations, leading to fasting-induced hyperuricemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral stage",
    "long_answer": "In classical Freudian theory, schizoid personality disorder is viewed as a fixation at the oral stage of psychosexual development (the first year of life). Disturbances in early oral experiences—feeding, sucking, and the infant’s primary attachment to the mother—are thought to hinder the formation of secure object relations. This early oral fixation is proposed to lead to the characteristic detachment, social withdrawal, and reliance on fantasy seen in schizoid personality disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Squamous cell carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "In Van der Woude syndrome, the congenital lower-lip pits are lined by stratified squamous epithelium that can be chronically inflamed by constant moisture and minor infection. Long-standing inflammation and epithelial turnover create a field for dysplastic change, and several documented cases show malignant transformation of the pit epithelium into squamous cell carcinoma of the lip/oral mucosa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lateral rectus",
    "long_answer": "Thiamine (vitamin B₁) deficiency can lead to Wernicke’s encephalopathy, whose classic ocular manifestation is ophthalmoparesis caused by abducens (cranial nerve VI) palsy. Paralysis of the abducens nerve specifically weakens the lateral rectus muscle, producing impaired abduction of the eye. Thus, the lateral rectus is the extra-ocular muscle characteristically involved in thiamine-deficiency–related ophthalmoparesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Probenecid",
    "long_answer": "Probenecid blocks the renal tubular secretion of organic acids, including penicillin, thereby prolonging penicillin’s serum half-life and raising cerebrospinal fluid levels. For neurosyphilis, oral probenecid is co-administered with aqueous penicillin G to ensure higher and sustained penicillin concentrations in the blood and CSF, optimizing treponemicidal efficacy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sulfate-reducing bacteria",
    "long_answer": "In aquatic sediments, anaerobic sulfate-reducing bacteria (e.g., Desulfovibrio, Desulfobacter spp.) possess the hgcAB gene pair that catalyzes conversion of inorganic Hg(II) to neurotoxic methylmercury. These SRB dominate mercury methylation under the low-oxygen, sulfate-rich conditions typical of freshwater and coastal environments, making them the primary biological agents of mercury methylation in aquatic systems."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greek",
    "long_answer": "The term “tachycardia” comes from the Greek words “tachys” (ταχύς), meaning “swift” or “rapid,” and “kardia” (καρδία), meaning “heart.” Thus, its linguistic origin is Greek, directly reflecting a condition in which the heart beats faster than normal at rest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inguinal hernia",
    "long_answer": "One of the earliest clinical manifestations of Hunter syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis II) is an inguinal hernia—the protrusion of abdominal contents through the inguinal canal. Accumulation of glycosaminoglycans weakens connective tissue and fascia, so many affected infants require surgical repair of an inguinal hernia before other characteristic signs of the disorder become evident."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pentasomy X",
    "long_answer": "Pentasomy X (karyotype 49,XXXXX) is a rare chromosomal disorder in which a female has five copies of the X chromosome instead of the usual two. Clinical features include characteristic craniofacial anomalies such as low-set ears, along with developmental delay, hypotonia, and short stature, making it the established diagnosis fitting the description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tacrolimus",
    "long_answer": "Topical tacrolimus (0.1% ointment) has been shown in multiple clinical reports to markedly reduce the inflammation and blistering of Hailey–Hailey disease. By inhibiting calcineurin-dependent T-cell activation and downstream cytokine release, tacrolimus lessens secondary inflammation and helps restore epidermal integrity, allowing long-term control of this inherited acantholytic dermatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "The Good Doctor",
    "long_answer": "The ABC medical drama The Good Doctor includes an episode in which a teenage patient—with recurrent joint dislocations, hyper-extensible skin and easy bruising—receives the definitive diagnosis of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. The storyline explicitly names the hereditary collagen-synthesis disorder and discusses its hallmark connective-tissue fragility, thereby bringing the condition to the attention of the viewing audience."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCNN1A",
    "long_answer": "OMIM notes that pathogenic variants in SCNN1A, which encodes the α-subunit of the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) and causes the systemic (autosomal-recessive) form of Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1, are also linked to a phenotype of female infertility. Impaired ENaC function in the endometrium disrupts uterine fluid absorption and embryo implantation, explaining the association with infertility in affected women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NMDA receptor antagonism",
    "long_answer": "Nitrous oxide produces visual and auditory hallucinations because it acts as a non-competitive antagonist at N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) glutamate receptors in the brain. By blocking NMDA-mediated excitatory neurotransmission, especially in cortical and limbic circuits that integrate sensory information, nitrous oxide induces a dissociative state that manifests as vivid perceptual distortions and hallucinations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Patent ductus arteriosus",
    "long_answer": "Maternal rubella infection during early pregnancy can produce congenital rubella syndrome, whose most characteristic cardiac anomaly is a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA). Follow-up studies of children with congenital rubella showed that those who later met criteria for autism spectrum disorder frequently had the same lesion, making PDA the birth defect most consistently associated with both congenital rubella syndrome and autism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Health",
    "long_answer": "In biomedical science, health is defined as the level of functional and metabolic efficiency of a living organism. It encompasses the capacity to maintain physiological balance, resist disease, and perform daily activities, thereby enabling the individual to live long, function effectively, and thrive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High-dose corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic retroperitoneal fibrosis is an immune-mediated fibro-inflammatory process. When there is no severe ureteric obstruction requiring immediate decompression, the first-line therapy is medical: a course of high-dose systemic corticosteroids (e.g., prednisone 0.75–1 mg/kg/day) to suppress inflammation and halt further fibrotic tissue formation before tapering over months."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Third & fourth pharyngeal pouches",
    "long_answer": "The human parathyroid glands originate from the endoderm of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches during embryogenesis—the inferior parathyroids from the third pouch (migrating with the thymus) and the superior parathyroids from the fourth pouch. Disorders such as hypoparathyroidism can therefore be traced back to derivatives of these specific pharyngeal pouches."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Substance P",
    "long_answer": "In complex regional pain syndrome, peripheral C-fiber nociceptors release the neuropeptide Substance P, which promotes neurogenic inflammation, vasodilation, plasma extravasation, and sensitization of spinal dorsal-horn neurons. Elevated local concentrations of Substance P have been demonstrated in CRPS-affected limbs, implicating this neuropeptide as a key mediator in the syndrome’s pathophysiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurologist",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CAPN3 gene cause limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2A/LGMDR1, a neuromuscular disorder. Evaluation, genetic confirmation, and management of muscular dystrophies are the responsibility of a neurologist—particularly one with expertise in neuromuscular diseases—who coordinates diagnosis, monitoring, physiotherapy, and supportive care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteomyology",
    "long_answer": "Osteomyology is a complementary manual-therapy system devised in 1992 by an English-trained doctor of osteopathy as a blend of osteopathic, chiropractic and soft-tissue techniques. Because the title \"osteomyologist\" is not protected by law, the practice falls outside the statutory remit of the UK General Osteopathic Council and therefore remains unregulated by that body."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bloom syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bloom syndrome is an autosomal-recessive chromosomal-instability disorder caused by biallelic loss of the BLM DNA-helicase gene. Patients are characteristically of marked short stature, show photosensitive facial telangiectasias, and have a very high lifetime risk of malignancy. Among the cancers that occur early and with greatly elevated frequency is acute lymphoblastic leukemia, making Bloom syndrome a well-established genetic condition that links short stature with a strong predisposition to ALL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abortion (miscarriage)",
    "long_answer": "Quinine directly stimulates smooth-muscle fibers of the gravid uterus. This oxytocic action can markedly increase uterine contractions, so therapeutic or higher doses of quinine may precipitate abortion (spontaneous miscarriage) in pregnant women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anxiety disorder",
    "long_answer": "Both tokophobia—a specific, intense fear of childbirth—and somatic symptom disorder commonly co-occur with anxiety disorders. Clinical studies repeatedly document heightened anxiety levels and diagnosable anxiety disorders (e.g., generalized anxiety disorder, specific phobia) in patients exhibiting either condition, underscoring anxiety disorder as the shared psychological comorbidity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX1",
    "long_answer": "In DiGeorge syndrome the 22q11.2 microdeletion removes one copy of the TBX1 gene. TBX1 encodes a T-box transcription factor that regulates development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches; its haploinsufficiency disrupts formation of the supporting stroma of both the thymus and parathyroid glands, leading to the characteristic thymic hypoplasia and hypocalcaemia seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA",
    "long_answer": "Malouf syndrome (dilated cardiomyopathy with hypergonadotropic hypogonadism) is most often caused by heterozygous mutations in the LMNA gene, which encodes the nuclear lamina proteins lamin A and lamin C. Lamins are integral to nuclear-envelope structure and take part in DNA replication, transcription and the overall regulation of protein biosynthesis, making LMNA the gene mutation associated with Malouf syndrome that is directly involved in protein biosynthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Raja clavata",
    "long_answer": "Documented cases report complete or partial albinism in the thornback ray, Raja clavata (family Rajidae). These individuals lack melanin in the skin and eyes, producing a pale-white appearance; the condition arises from loss-of-function mutations in melanin-biosynthetic pathways, a phenomenon recorded sporadically in elasmobranchs including this skate species."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenocopy",
    "long_answer": "A phenocopy is an environmentally induced variation that produces the same phenotype as a mutation-based (genetic) disorder, even though the individual lacks the causative mutant allele. Environmental factors such as drugs, nutrition, infection, or temperature create a phenotype that ‘copies’ the inherited condition, making it appear genetic when it is not."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric neurology",
    "long_answer": "Satoyoshi syndrome presents in childhood with painful, progressive muscle spasms, neuromuscular hyperexcitability and other systemic signs. Because the core manifestations are neurological and begin early in life, children with this rare autoimmune condition are typically evaluated and managed by pediatric neurologists, who specialize in diagnosing and treating neurologic and neuromuscular disorders in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Unreliable pediatric spirometry",
    "long_answer": "A firm asthma diagnosis usually relies on objective lung-function measures such as spirometry, but children under about 5–6 years cannot perform the forced-exhalation maneuvers consistently. Because definitive pulmonary function data are hard to obtain, physicians may avoid assigning the asthma label and instead treat presumptive airway inflammation empirically with inhaled or systemic corticosteroids."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TSEN54",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TSEN54 gene, which encodes a subunit of the tRNA-splicing endonuclease complex, are the classic cause of pontocerebellar hypoplasia type 2. Affected individuals show severe cerebellar and pontine hypoplasia together with characteristic dysmorphic facial features (e.g., high nasal bridge, long philtrum, micrognathia, low-set ears). Thus, TSEN54 is the gene whose mutation leads to pontocerebellar hypoplasia accompanied by dysmorphic features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cleft palate",
    "long_answer": "Tsao-Ellingson syndrome is a rare multiple-anomaly disorder whose case reports consistently describe midline palatal clefting. Cleft palate is therefore considered one of the most characteristic and frequently observed facial findings in affected individuals, alongside micrognathia and ear anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genetic counseling",
    "long_answer": "Medical genetics contributes to Eisenmenger-syndrome care mainly by providing genetic counseling: assessing whether the patient’s underlying congenital heart defect is part of an inherited or syndromic condition, arranging targeted genetic testing, informing patients and relatives about recurrence risk, and guiding reproductive planning and prenatal diagnosis. This helps identify at-risk family members and supports informed clinical and family-planning decisions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maurice Parsonage, John Aldren Turner",
    "long_answer": "Parsonage–Turner syndrome, also called neuralgic amyotrophy, was first delineated in 1948 by British neurologists Maurice Parsonage and John Aldren Turner. Their landmark case series in The Lancet described the acute, painful brachial neuritis and subsequent muscle weakness that now bears their combined eponym."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ICP47-mediated TAP inhibition",
    "long_answer": "Herpes simplex virus encodes the immediate-early protein ICP47, which binds to the transporter associated with antigen processing (TAP) in the endoplasmic reticulum. This blocks TAP from translocating viral peptides into the ER lumen, preventing loading of peptides onto MHC class I molecules. As a result, surface MHC I expression and antigen presentation to cytotoxic T lymphocytes are greatly reduced, allowing HSV-infected cells to escape CTL recognition and killing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orchiectomy",
    "long_answer": "SBMA (Kennedy’s disease) is driven by androgen-dependent toxicity of the mutant androgen-receptor protein. Surgically removing the testes through bilateral orchiectomy eliminates the major source of testosterone, thereby lowering androgen stimulation of the mutant receptor and has been shown in case reports and animal models to slow clinical progression of weakness and atrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HPSE2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Urofacial (Ochoa) syndrome is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HPSE2 gene (encoding heparanase-2). The defective bladder innervation produced by HPSE2 mutations leads to functional obstruction, urinary stasis and vesicoureteral reflux, predisposing affected patients to recurrent urinary tract infections and upper-tract involvement such as pyelonephritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention",
    "long_answer": "During the early stages of the 2009 H1N1 swine-flu outbreak, officials from the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) publicly projected that the novel H1N1 virus could eventually infect roughly 30 % of the U.S. population—about one in three Americans—based on historical pandemic patterns and early transmission data."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skull X-ray",
    "long_answer": "A plain radiograph of the skull (skull X-ray) can demonstrate premature fusion of the cranial sutures, thereby confirming the diagnosis of Apert syndrome, which is characterized by craniosynostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Planum temporale",
    "long_answer": "In most people the planum temporale—an auditory–language area on the superior temporal lobe—is larger on the left hemisphere. MRI studies show that children with mixed receptive-expressive language disorder often lack this normal left-greater-than-right asymmetry (or show a reversed asymmetry), indicating atypical lateralization of the planum temporale that correlates with their language comprehension and production deficits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alexithymia",
    "long_answer": "Alexithymia is a neuro-psychological trait in which a person has marked difficulty identifying, describing, and outwardly expressing his or her own emotions and in recognizing the emotions of others. Because emotional information fails to reach conscious awareness, investigators have likened it to an affective “scotoma,” analogous to a blind spot in the visual field—hence the link sometimes drawn between alexithymia and visual scotomas. The term therefore precisely denotes the inability to perceive or communicate emotion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Insensitivity to pain",
    "long_answer": "Kurukawa-Takagi-Nakao syndrome belongs to the group of hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies; its cardinal sensory feature is a congenital inability to perceive painful stimuli. Affected individuals do not feel pain despite tissue-damaging events, leading to repeated injuries and self-mutilation. This loss of pain perception is the characteristic sensory symptom associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antibiotics",
    "long_answer": "Although sarcoidosis is not an infectious disease, certain antibiotics—most commonly tetracycline derivatives such as minocycline or doxycycline—are sometimes prescribed. Beyond their antimicrobial activity, these drugs possess anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory properties that can help reduce the granulomatous lesions characteristic of sarcoidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ireland",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological data place Ireland among the nations with the highest age-standardised incidence rates of oesophageal adenocarcinoma. The country’s temperate maritime climate, marked by mild temperatures and abundant rainfall, fosters rich green landscapes—hence its nickname, the “Emerald Isle.” This combination of a documented high burden of oesophageal adenocarcinoma and a reputation for lush vegetation and a mild climate precisely identifies Ireland."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Post-stroke depression",
    "long_answer": "A considerable proportion of stroke survivors develop post-stroke depression; the mood disturbance reduces motivation and interest in activities. Even when motor capacity is sufficient for tasks such as walking, depressive symptoms (anhedonia, low energy, apathy) lead to poor engagement in therapy, thereby complicating rehabilitation outcomes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methyl isocyanate",
    "long_answer": "Methyl isocyanate (MIC) is a highly volatile isocyanate intermediate used to manufacture carbamate pesticides such as carbaryl. When inhaled, MIC’s strong alkylating properties cause acute injury to the bronchiolar epithelium and intense inflammatory and fibrotic responses, which can progress to the fixed airflow obstruction characteristic of bronchiolitis obliterans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Albinism group",
    "long_answer": "Cross syndrome is an oculocerebral hypopigmentation disorder characterized by generalized cutaneous and ocular albinism together with neurologic defects. It is therefore classified among the hereditary pigmentary disorders of the albinism group of genodermatoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Germany",
    "long_answer": "Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (MCADD) is considerably more frequent in populations of northern-European ancestry. Newborn-screening data show the highest national incidence in Germany, where the disorder is detected in roughly 1 in 5,000–7,000 live births—higher than the rates reported from the United Kingdom, the United States, or other screened populations. This elevated frequency reflects the high carrier prevalence of the common ACADM gene mutation c.985A>G in the German population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Mandibuloacral dysplasia results from biallelic (homozygous or compound-heterozygous) pathogenic variants—most commonly in LMNA or ZMPSTE24. Because two mutated alleles are required for expression and carrier parents are usually unaffected, the disorder is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Varicella (chickenpox)",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies show that children who develop varicella (chickenpox) have a several-fold higher incidence of arterial ischemic stroke during the subsequent 6–12 months. Varicella-zoster virus can infect and inflame cerebral arteries, producing a post-varicella arteriopathy that predisposes to thrombosis and stroke, making this common childhood illness a recognized trigger for pediatric arterial ischemic stroke in the year after infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TUBB4A mutation",
    "long_answer": "A pathogenic variant in the TUBB4A gene (DYT-4 dystonia) produces a clinical picture that includes primary torsion dystonia which can generalise and a characteristic laryngeal dystonia manifested as spasmodic (\"whispering\") dysphonia. Thus the same TUBB4A mutation is linked to both torsion dystonia and spasmodic dysphonia in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Lymphomatoid papulosis is a CD30-positive cutaneous T-cell lymphoproliferative disorder that, in a minority of patients, can progress to systemic malignant lymphoma. The most feared and clinically aggressive transformation is into systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma (ALCL), a high-grade T-cell lymphoma characterized by CD30 expression and rapid clinical course."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic",
    "long_answer": "In medical terminology, a condition whose underlying cause is unknown is described as idiopathic. Historically, the term “idiopathic hyperoxaluria” was sometimes used interchangeably with “primary hyperoxaluria,” reflecting the fact that the etiology of the excessive oxalate production was not yet understood. Hence, idiopathic is the descriptor for a disease of unknown cause."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spiral ligament",
    "long_answer": "In progressive familial vestibulocochlear dysfunction (DFNA9 caused by COCH-gene mutations), a PAS-positive mucopolysaccharide-rich (cochlin) material accumulates in the spiral ligament of the cochlear lateral wall; this deposition disrupts cochlear mechanics and vestibular end-organ function, producing the characteristic progressive hearing loss and vestibular symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Bartsocas-Papas syndrome is an autosomal-recessive multiple-congenital-anomaly disorder (lethal popliteal pterygium syndrome). Diagnosis relies on dysmorphologic examination and molecular genetic testing, and care centers on recurrence-risk counseling and coordination of multispecialty management. These tasks are the domain of clinical/medical geneticists, the specialty dedicated to diagnosing and treating hereditary syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "William Dameshek",
    "long_answer": "In 1951 the hematologist William Paul Dameshek introduced the term “myeloproliferative disorders” to group together chronic myeloid leukemia, polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis (then referred to as myelofibrosis with myeloid metaplasia). By doing so, Dameshek was the first to characterize primary myelofibrosis as a myeloproliferative condition driven by clonal proliferation of hematopoietic stem cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyproheptadine",
    "long_answer": "Painful bruising syndrome (Gardner–Diamond or auto-erythrocyte sensitization syndrome) has no universally effective therapy, but several case reports and small series describe complete, sustained remission after treatment with the serotonin-antagonist antihistamine cyproheptadine. Daily oral cyproheptadine (8–12 mg) is thought to stabilize capillary membranes and counteract vasoactive amines implicated in the syndrome, leading to disappearance of the recurrent painful ecchymoses and prolonged remission once the drug is withdrawn."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydrolethalus syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Katz syndrome is the eponymous name for hydrolethalus syndrome type 2, a lethal multiple-congenital-anomaly condition characterized by severe hydrocephalus, midline brain defects and limb malformations. Hence, it is classified as a hydrolethalus syndrome birth defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchogenic carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "A primary lung tumor such as bronchogenic carcinoma can grow into or externally compress the central pulmonary veins. This tumoral invasion or mass effect narrows the venous lumen, producing acquired pulmonary vein stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bethlem myopathy 2",
    "long_answer": "Kyphosis is a documented axial-skeletal manifestation of Bethlem myopathy type 2, the form caused by pathogenic variants in the COL12A1 gene. Patients with BTHLM2 often show early joint hyperlaxity that progresses to contractures together with spinal deformities such as scoliosis and kyphosis, distinguishing it from the more common COL6-related Bethlem myopathy type 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neural cell adhesion molecule",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary inclusion body myopathy (GNE myopathy) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the GNE enzyme that synthesises sialic acid. The resulting lack of sialic acid leads to hypoglycosylation of several muscle glycoproteins; the most consistently and markedly affected is the neural cell adhesion molecule (NCAM), which shows reduced sialic-acid content (under-sialylation) in muscle biopsies from patients and is widely used as a biochemical marker for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Bell’s palsy (facial nerve palsy) can occur after surgery in the parotid region because the facial nerve passes through the gland. The first-line, evidence-based treatment for Bell’s palsy is a short course of oral corticosteroids—most commonly prednisone—which reduces nerve inflammation and improves the likelihood and speed of full functional recovery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Extremely rare (<5%)",
    "long_answer": "Thymomas almost always remain confined to the mediastinum or spread locally to pleura and pericardium. Hematogenous distant metastasis (stage IVb) occurs in only about 3–5 % of thymoma cases, and among those few, bone involvement has been reported almost exclusively in isolated case reports. Therefore, the probability of a thymoma metastasizing to bone is considered extremely rare—well under 5 % of all thymoma presentations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematoxylin–eosin stain",
    "long_answer": "In Birt–Hogg–Dubé syndrome, the skin lesions are confirmed as fibrofolliculomas by routine histopathology. A punch or shave biopsy processed with standard hematoxylin–eosin (H&E) staining demonstrates the hallmark features—cystically dilated follicular infundibula with thin epithelial strands radiating into a perifollicular fibrous stroma—thereby confirming the presence of fibrofolliculomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Preferential inactivation of mutant allele",
    "long_answer": "In heterozygous females, X-inactivation is usually random, but if it is skewed so that the X chromosome carrying the pathogenic mutation is preferentially inactivated, far fewer cells express the mutant gene. This lowers the overall burden of dysfunctional protein, resulting in a markedly milder (or even subclinical) manifestation of an X-linked dominant disorder; conversely, skewing toward inactivation of the normal X makes disease more severe. Thus, the direction of skewed X-inactivation modulates phenotypic severity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infliximab",
    "long_answer": "Infliximab is a monoclonal antibody that neutralizes tumor-necrosis factor-α (TNF-α). It has been reported to control inflammation and bone lesions in chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis and is an FDA-approved biologic for inducing and maintaining remission in Crohn’s disease, underscoring its therapeutic role in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD34",
    "long_answer": "Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans, a malignant fibro-histiocytic skin tumor that can be mistaken for a benign dermatofibroma, characteristically shows strong, diffuse immunohistochemical positivity for the cell-surface marker CD34, whereas ordinary dermatofibromas are CD34-negative. Therefore, CD34 staining is used to identify and confirm the malignant lesion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Empty sella syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Primary (congenital) empty sella syndrome results from a developmental defect of the diaphragma sellae. After a traumatic brain injury, cerebrospinal-fluid pulsations can herniate the subarachnoid space into the sella turcica, compressing the pituitary gland and producing hypopituitarism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "Both leukemia and COVID-19 frequently lead to thrombocytopenia—a reduction in circulating platelets. In leukemia, malignant infiltration of the bone marrow suppresses normal megakaryocyte function, lowering platelet production. In COVID-19, platelets are depleted by inflammatory consumption, immune-mediated destruction, and marrow suppression from systemic infection. The shared outcome of these distinct mechanisms is a clinically significant drop in platelet count, i.e., thrombocytopenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "20/40 or better",
    "long_answer": "APMPPE is usually self-limited, and in most series 80–90 % of affected eyes regain visual acuity of 20/40 or better—often reaching 20/25-20/20—within weeks to a few months after the acute episode."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1",
    "long_answer": "Loose anagen syndrome has been repeatedly described in patients with Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1). NF1 is an autosomal-dominant RAS-pathway disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene and is classically characterized by multiple neurofibromas and other tumors of the nervous system (e.g., optic pathway gliomas, malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors). Several case reports and series document that children with NF1 can present with easily pluckable, sparse hair typical of loose anagen syndrome, linking this hair abnormality to the neurocutaneous tumor-predisposition condition NF1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHMP2B",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in CHMP2B (charged multivesicular-body protein 2B) are a recognised cause of autosomal-dominant hereditary spastic paraplegia (designated SPG82) and were originally identified in families with frontotemporal dementia (FTD3). Thus, the same CHMP2B mutations can underlie both hereditary spastic paraplegia and frontotemporal dementia through disruption of the ESCRT-III endosomal sorting pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cell-mediated immunity",
    "long_answer": "Congenital self-healing reticulohistiocytosis (Hashimoto-Pritzker disease) is a form of Langerhans cell histiocytosis. The disorder is characterized by proliferation of epidermal Langerhans cells, which are antigen-presenting dendritic cells integral to T-lymphocyte–driven (cell-mediated) immune reactions. Hence, the pathogenesis and lesion regression in this condition primarily involve a cell-mediated immune response rather than an antibody-mediated one."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Agonal respiration",
    "long_answer": "Agonal respiration—irregular, gasping breaths that occur when the brainstem is transiently active despite loss of effective circulation—is commonly seen in the first minutes of cardiac arrest. These gasps signal severe cardiovascular collapse and impending respiratory system failure, indicating that the patient is in true cardiac arrest and requires immediate resuscitation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genetic disorder",
    "long_answer": "FACES syndrome is a rare multiple-congenital-anomaly syndrome present from birth and thought to follow autosomal-recessive inheritance. Because it results from underlying genetic causes rather than environmental or purely developmental factors, it is classified as a genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oculocutaneous albinism",
    "long_answer": "Cross syndrome, also called oculocerebral hypopigmentation (Cross type), is characterized by generalized hypopigmentation of the skin and hair together with ocular and neurologic defects. Because it results from inherited defects in melanin biosynthesis, it is classed as a variant of oculocutaneous albinism, an autosomal-recessive genodermatosis."
}
